.TH C5XASM 1 "2026-10-05" "c5xtools 1.0.1" "c5xtools Manual"
.SH NAME
c5xasm \- assembler for the TMS320C5x fixed-point DSP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c5xasm
.I input.asm
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-coff | \-coff1 | \-coff2 ]
.RB [ \-v50 ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-q | \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c5xasm
translates one TMS320C5x ('C5x) assembly-language source file into either a
relocatable TI COFF object (suitable for linking with
.BR c5xlnk (1))
or a flat, human-readable listing of the assembled machine words.
.PP
The complete 'C5x instruction set is accepted, together with sections and the
common assembler directives: data definition
.RB ( .word ", " .long ", " .string ", " .byte ),
storage
.RB ( .set ", " .space ", " .bss ),
macros
.RB ( .macro ", " .endm ,
with positional parameters), conditional assembly
.RB ( .if ", " .elseif ", " .else ", " .endif ),
block repetition
.RB ( .loop ", " .endloop )
and substitution symbols
.RB ( .asg ", " .eval ).
.PP
Symbol addresses are resolved by repeated sizing passes that relax to a fixed
point, so forward references and span-dependent short/long immediate forms
(for example
.BR ADDK " versus " ADLK )
converge automatically before the final pass emits the output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I input.asm
The assembly source file to translate. Exactly one input file is required; it
is the only positional argument.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the result to
.IR file .
With no COFF flag the flat listing goes to standard output by default, so
.B \-o
is optional; when a COFF object is requested
.RB ( \-coff ", " \-coff1 ", " \-coff2 )
an output file is required because the object is binary. This option selects
only the destination; it does not change the generated content.
.TP
.BR \-coff ", " \-coff2
Emit a TI COFF version\ 2 relocatable object (22-byte file header, 48-byte
section headers). This is the default COFF revision and the normal choice for
producing an object to hand to
.BR c5xlnk (1).
Selecting it switches the output from the flat listing to a binary object that
carries sections, a symbol table and relocations.
.TP
.B \-coff1
Emit a TI COFF version\ 1 object (22-byte file header, 40-byte section
headers). The encoded code and data are identical to
.BR \-coff2 ;
only the container layout is the older revision. Use it when a downstream
consumer expects COFF\ v1.
.TP
.B \-v50
Select the 'C5x instruction set. 'C5x is the only supported target, so this
flag is accepted for command-line compatibility and otherwise has no effect.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-verbose
Print a progress report to standard error: the number of relaxation passes,
the count of words emitted, the number of relocations generated and the output
mode. Diagnostic only; it does not alter the object.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Reduce the start-up identity banner (printed to standard error) to a single
line instead of the full banner. It affects only that banner, never the
assembled output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and identity banner to standard output and exit
successfully.
.PP
When no COFF flag is given, the default output is the flat listing: one
.RI ' "SEC AAAA WWWW" '
line per emitted word, giving the section tag, the 16-bit address and the
16-bit value. It is meant for inspection and diffing and is not linkable.
.SH EXAMPLES
Assemble a source file into a COFF object ready for linking:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm fir.asm \-coff \-o fir.obj
.EE
.PP
Inspect the assembled words without producing an object (flat listing to the
terminal):
.PP
.EX
c5xasm fir.asm
.EE
.PP
Produce an older COFF version\ 1 object and report what was emitted:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm fir.asm \-coff1 \-o fir.obj \-V
.EE
.SS Full toolchain flow
The three tools chain together: assemble each source to a COFF object with
.BR c5xasm (1),
link the objects into an image with
.BR c5xlnk (1),
then turn that image into a ROM or programmer format such as Intel-HEX with
.BR c5xhex (1).
.PP
Absolute build (the usual case: fixed load addresses, directly loadable),
ending in a standard Intel-HEX file:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm main.asm \-coff \-o main.obj  # source -> COFF object
c5xasm dsp.asm  \-coff \-o dsp.obj
c5xlnk main.obj dsp.obj \-o app.out  # objects -> absolute .out
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-o app.hex  # .out -> Intel-HEX image
.EE
.PP
Relocatable build (partially link now, finish the absolute link later):
.PP
.EX
c5xasm a.asm \-coff \-o a.obj
c5xasm b.asm \-coff \-o b.obj
c5xlnk a.obj b.obj \-r \-o part.obj   # relocatable, re-linkable
c5xlnk part.obj \-o app.out          # final absolute link
c5xhex app.out \-f srec \-o app.s19   # S-record alternative
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An input/output error or an assembly error (for example an undefined symbol or
an operand that does not fit its field).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: missing input file, or an unknown or malformed option.
.SH NOTES
A COFF object produced with
.B \-coff
is more than the assembled words: it carries named sections, a symbol table
and relocation entries that record every place an address must be patched once
the final load address is known. That is why an object can be linked, while
the flat listing \- which is only the finished words at their addresses, with
no relocation information \- cannot.
.PP
Because some instructions have both a short and a long immediate form (which
differ in size), and because a label may be used before it is defined,
.B c5xasm
cannot know all addresses in one pass. It sizes the program repeatedly until
the addresses stop moving (a fixed point), then emits once. This is why
forward references and span-dependent forms resolve in a single invocation
without any manual fix-up.
.PP
Code is placed in
.BR .text ,
initialised data in
.BR .data ,
and space reserved but not initialised in
.BR .bss .
The assembler only tags the words with their section; the linker later decides
the actual addresses (see
.BR c5xlnk (1)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR c5xlnk (1),
.BR c5xhex (1),
.BR c5xdis (1)
